## Solution

A Brownian bridge $(X_t)_{t \in [0,1]}$ is a Gaussian process with $X_0 = X_1 = 0$ and covariance
$$\operatorname{Cov}(X_s, X_t) = \min(s,t) - st.$$

For $s < t < 1$, the conditional distribution of $X_t$ given $X_s = x$ is Gaussian with

$$E[X_t \mid X_s = x] = \frac{\operatorname{Cov}(X_s, X_t)}{\operatorname{Var}(X_s)}\,x = \frac{s(1-t)}{s(1-s)}\,x = \frac{1-t}{1-s}\,x,$$

and

$$\operatorname{Var}(X_t \mid X_s = x) = \operatorname{Var}(X_t) - \frac{\operatorname{Cov}(X_s,X_t)^2}{\operatorname{Var}(X_s)} = t(1-t) - \frac{s(1-t)^2}{1-s} = \frac{(t-s)(1-t)}{1-s}.$$

Setting $s = \tfrac{3}{5}$ and $t = \tfrac{4}{5}$:

$$E\!\left[X_{4/5} \mid X_{3/5}\right] = \frac{1-\tfrac{4}{5}}{1-\tfrac{3}{5}}\,X_{3/5} = \frac{\tfrac{1}{5}}{\tfrac{2}{5}}\,X_{3/5} = \tfrac{1}{2}\,X_{3/5},$$

$$\operatorname{Var}\!\left(X_{4/5} \mid X_{3/5}\right) = \frac{(\tfrac{4}{5}-\tfrac{3}{5})(1-\tfrac{4}{5})}{1-\tfrac{3}{5}} = \frac{(\tfrac{1}{5})(\tfrac{1}{5})}{\tfrac{2}{5}} = \frac{\tfrac{1}{25}}{\tfrac{2}{5}} = \frac{1}{10}.$$

Therefore, conditional on $X_{3/5}$, the random variable
$$X_{4/5} - \tfrac{1}{2}X_{3/5}$$
is Gaussian with mean $0$ and variance $\tfrac{1}{10}$.

Using the characteristic function of a centered Gaussian, $E[e^{iuZ}] = e^{-\frac{1}{2}\sigma^2 u^2}$ for $Z \sim \mathcal{N}(0,\sigma^2)$:

$$E\!\left[e^{iu\left(X_{4/5}-\frac{1}{2}X_{3/5}\right)} \,\middle|\, X_{3/5}\right] = \exp\!\left(-\tfrac{1}{2}\cdot\tfrac{1}{10}\cdot u^2\right) = \boxed{\,e^{-u^2/20}\,}.$$

Note the result is deterministic (non-random) — it does not depend on the value of $X_{3/5}$, since the conditional mean of $X_{4/5}$ exactly cancels the $\tfrac{1}{2}X_{3/5}$ term.

